mkvenv hands pip its bundled wheels as a real file: URL.

configure runs `mkvenv ensuregroup --dir <source>/python/wheels`, and
mkvenv passed that to pip as f"file://{wheels_dir}". On Windows the
directory is C:/..., so the URL is file://C:/..., whose host is "C:".
Python 3.13's url2pathname took the drive letter out of it anyway;
3.14's reads it as written, a UNC path \\C:\..., and pip finds no wheels:
"could not find a version that satisfies requirement pycotap==1.3.1 ...
mkvenv was configured to operate offline". This is the native Windows
build (MSYS2 MINGW64, docs/build-windows.md), whose only Python is 3.14,
2026-09-17. Path.as_uri() gives file:///C:/... there and the same
file:///... URL as before everywhere else.

diff --git a/python/scripts/mkvenv.py b/python/scripts/mkvenv.py
--- a/python/scripts/mkvenv.py
+++ b/python/scripts/mkvenv.py
@@ -615,7 +615,10 @@
     if not online:
         full_args += ["--no-index"]
     if wheels_dir:
-        full_args += ["--find-links", f"file://{str(wheels_dir)}"]
+        # A URL, not "file://" + a path: on Windows the path starts with a
+        # drive, and file://C:/... names a host "C:". Python 3.14's
+        # url2pathname reads it that way (\\C:\...); older ones guessed.
+        full_args += ["--find-links", Path(wheels_dir).resolve().as_uri()]
     full_args += list(args)
     subprocess.run(
         full_args,
